course: history
lesson: 25
title: Printing, the Renaissance and the Reformation
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Renaissance, which means rebirth, began in Italy in the 1400s, when artists and thinkers grew excited again about the art and learning of ancient Greece and Rome.
explain: Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg in Germany built a printing press with movable metal letters; before that, every European book was copied by hand, like writing out a whole Harry Potter book with a pen.
explain: Printing in China and Korea was even older, but Gutenberg's press spread ideas across Europe so fast that nobody could stop them, which is why Martin Luther's criticism of the Church in 1517 split western Christianity.
explain: This was the age of Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, of Shakespeare writing plays for London's Globe theatre, of Elizabeth I's ships beating the Spanish Armada, and of Suleiman the Magnificent ruling the mighty Ottoman Empire.
fact: The Renaissance, meaning rebirth, began in Italy in the 1400s with new excitement about Greek and Roman learning and art.
fact: Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg in Germany built a printing press with movable metal letters that could be reused.
fact: Printing was older in East Asia: the Chinese printed with carved wooden blocks, and Korea printed a book with metal type in 1377.
fact: Before printing, books in Europe were copied by hand, so they were rare and very expensive; within about fifty years of Gutenberg millions of books had been printed.
fact: Leonardo da Vinci painted the Mona Lisa, studied the human body and drew ideas for flying machines in notebooks written in mirror writing, though most of his machines were never built and many would not have worked.
fact: Michelangelo carved the statue of David and painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome over about four years, standing on scaffolding and leaning back, not lying down as the legend says.
fact: In 1517 Martin Luther, a German monk, criticised the Church for selling forgiveness, and printing spread his ideas quickly.
fact: The Reformation split western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches, which led to long wars and later to more religious freedom.
fact: Reformers translated the Bible from Latin into German, English and other languages so ordinary people could read it themselves.
fact: William Shakespeare wrote plays such as Romeo and Juliet, Hamlet and Macbeth around 1600 for the Globe theatre in London, and he first wrote down many words and phrases still used today, such as wild-goose chase.
fact: Elizabeth I ruled England for 45 years, and in 1588 her navy and stormy weather defeated the Spanish Armada.
fact: Suleiman the Magnificent ruled the Ottoman Empire at its height in the 1500s, from Hungary to Iraq and North Africa, and was famous for his laws and great mosques.
exercise: What does Renaissance mean? | Rebirth | Revolution | Great war | New king
exercise: In which country did the Renaissance begin? | Italy | England | Mongolia | Mali
exercise: What was special about Gutenberg's letters? | They were movable metal letters that could be reused | They were painted by hand on every page by monks in a hurry | They were made of chocolate | They could only print one word
exercise: About when did Gutenberg build his press? | Around 1450 | Around 1066 | Around 1900 | Around 800
exercise: Where was printing done even before Gutenberg? | In China and Korea | Nowhere in the world | Only in Viking Norway | In Australia
exercise: How did printing help ideas spread? | Books became cheap and quick to make | It made people forget how to read and write | It stopped people writing letters to each other | It made books more rare
exercise: Which famous painting did Leonardo da Vinci paint? | The Mona Lisa | The Sistine Chapel ceiling | The Bayeux Tapestry | The Book of Kells
exercise: How did Leonardo write in his notebooks? | In mirror writing, from right to left | Only in numbers and secret codes | In Chinese characters he learned in Venice | He never wrote anything
exercise: What did Michelangelo paint? | The ceiling of the Sistine Chapel | The Mona Lisa | The walls of the Alhambra | The Globe theatre
exercise: What did Martin Luther criticise in 1517? | The Church selling forgiveness | The price of bread in the markets of Germany | The English king | The Ottoman navy
exercise: How did printing help Luther? | His ideas were printed and spread quickly | He printed money to pay soldiers to fight for him | It made him the Pope | It let him travel faster
exercise: Into which two main churches did western Christianity split? | Catholic and Protestant | Orthodox and Buddhist | Viking and Norman | Muslim and Jewish
exercise: Why was a Bible in your own language important? | Ordinary people could read it themselves | It was cheaper to burn on cold winter nights | Latin was banned everywhere in Europe by law | It had more pages
exercise: Name a play by Shakespeare. | Hamlet | The Odyssey | Don Quixote | The Canterbury Tales
exercise: What was the Spanish Armada? | A Spanish fleet that tried to invade England | A Spanish castle on the coast | A famous Spanish dance with fans and castanets | A Spanish book of laws
exercise: Which empire did Suleiman the Magnificent rule? | The Ottoman Empire | The Mongol Empire | The Roman Empire | The British Empire
test: my nan says michelangelo painted the ceiling lying flat on his back for years. right? | No, he stood and leaned back on scaffolding | Yes, lying flat for twenty years | No, he painted it on the floor and lifted it up | No, Leonardo painted it
test: A friend says Gutenberg invented printing, full stop. What is more accurate? | Printing was older in China and Korea; he built a movable-type press in Europe | He invented printing, paper and ink all by himself in one single year | Printing was invented by the Romans | Nobody printed anything until 1900
test: Before printing, how long would it take one person to make a copy of a long book? | Months, copying every word by hand | About ten minutes with a quill and fast hands | One second | It could never be done
test: In a game you see Leonardo's flying machine bombing castles. What should you put right? | Most of his machines were never built and many would not have worked | His flying machine won many wars | He built hundreds of them | Leonardo invented the aeroplane in 1903
test: What would Luther's ideas have done without the printing press? | Spread far more slowly | Spread even faster | Turned into Latin by magic | Stopped the Renaissance from ever happening
test: Ben says Elizabeth I beat the Armada all on her own with a sword. What really happened? | Her navy and stormy weather defeated it | She fought it alone on horseback with her own sword | The Armada never sailed | France beat it for her
test: the ottomans in EU are always huge, is that just the game being weird? | No, they really were one of the strongest states in the world | Yes, they were tiny in real life | Yes, they never existed | No, they ruled America too
test: You say 'wild-goose chase' or 'the green-eyed monster'. Which writer first wrote such phrases down? | Shakespeare | Martin Luther | Gutenberg | Suleiman
test: Why did so many more people learn to read after printing? | Books became cheap and easy to get | Schools were banned | Reading was made easier by the Pope | Everyone was given a free computer
test: The Reformation led to long wars. What good thing did it also lead to later? | More religious freedom | The end of all books | The fall of Constantinople | The Black Death
exam: Identify the meaning of the term Renaissance. | Rebirth | Reformation | Revolution | Restoration of the monarchy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the innovation introduced by Johannes Gutenberg circa fourteen hundred and fifty? | A printing press employing movable metal type | The first printing of any kind in the world | The manufacture of paper from silk | The hand-copying of manuscripts
exam: Ascertain the principal grievance articulated by Martin Luther in fifteen hundred and seventeen. | The sale of forgiveness by the Church | The taxation of tea | The invasion of England by Spain and Portugal | The building of the Sistine Chapel ceiling
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the defeat of the Spanish Armada in fifteen hundred and eighty-eight. | It was effected by the English navy together with adverse weather | It was effected by the Ottoman fleet | The Armada was victorious | No engagement took place
